README.OSK -- what this port needed, and what it is===================================================Decus CPP is Martin Minow's public-domain C preprocessor, 1984-85,distributed on net.sources.  Its own readme opens "Decus cpp is a public-domain implementation of the C preprocessor", and that is the whole of itsterms.THIS COPY WAS ALREADY ADAPTED FOR OS-9 when it reached us, and that is whyit is here rather than any of the other Decus CPP copies about: `cppdef.h'defines SYS_OSK as a host, `cpp4.c' has an `#ifdef OSK' section, and`cpp1.c' writes Microware's own `#P' and `#5' line markers instead of`#line'.  It was built to stand in for Microware's cpp pass, and `-A'turns the markers off and gives ordinary `#line' output.WHAT HAD TO CHANGE HERE: one declaration, in cpp.h.    #ifndef OSK    extern char	*sprintf();		/* Lint needs this		*/    #endifMicroware's <stdio.h> declares sprintf() returning int, as K&R C does, andthe compiler stops with "**** declaration mismatch ****" rather thanwarning.  The line is there for lint's benefit on the systems the authorhad; nothing in cpp uses the return value.  Guarding it is the smallestchange that builds, and it is the ONLY change: everything else is as thesource arrived.Two warnings remain and are the author's, not ours -- `rindex' usedwithout a declaration in cpp1.c, twice, which K&R C reads as returningint.  Both results are only tested against zero and assigned to char*, soon a machine where the two are the same width it works, and this one is.Left alone deliberately.BUILD:  the recipe in tools/rebuild/recipes.psv, which is    cpp|cpp|cpp1.c cpp2.c cpp3.c cpp4.c cpp5.c cpp6.c|OSK||IT SHARES A NAME WITH A UTILITY YOU MAY ALREADY HAVE.  Microware ships a`cpp' as the compiler's preprocessor pass.  This one is not that one, andits card says so; `tools/shadowed-names.txt' is the list that keeps everysuch card honest.